problem_statement:
Testing request with mockk using custom body results in ClassCastException.
**Actual behavior (the bug)**
When trying to test Javalin API using Mockk with a request that uses bodyAsClass() throws ClassCastException

Test file
```kt
    @Test
    @Order(1)
    fun `create user with invalid short username`() {
        every {
            ctx.body()
        } returns jacksonObjectMapper().writeValueAsString(UserCreateBody("d", UserConstants.email, UserConstants.password))

        uc.createUser(ctx) // Line 50

        verify {
            ctx.status(201)
        }
    }
```
UserController (uc)
```kt
fun createUser(ctx: Context) {
        // ... 
        val body = ctx.bodyAsClass<UserCreateBody>() // This is what breaks (Line 45)
       // ...
}
```


Full Exception:

```kt
class java.lang.Object cannot be cast to class app.lightable.arc.domain.body.User.UserCreateBody (java.lang.Object is in module java.base of loader 'bootstrap'; app.lightable.arc.domain.body.User.UserCreateBody is in unnamed module of loader 'app')
java.lang.ClassCastException: class java.lang.Object cannot be cast to class app.lightable.arc.domain.body.User.UserCreateBody (java.lang.Object is in module java.base of loader 'bootstrap'; app.lightable.arc.domain.body.User.UserCreateBody is in unnamed module of loader 'app')
	at app.lightable.arc.domain.controllers.UserController.createUser(UserController.kt:45)
	at UserControllerTest.create user with invalid short username(UserControllerTest.kt:50)
```

**Expected behavior**
You shouldn't get a ClassCastException

**To Reproduce**

RequestBody.kt
```kt
data class ExampleBody(val myData: String)
```

ExampleController.kt
```kt
class ExampleController {
    fun thisWillBreak(ctx: Context) {
        val body = ctx.bodyAsClass<ExampleBody>()

        println("Data is ${body.myData}")

        ctx.status(200)
        return
    }
}
```

ExampleControllerTest.kt
```kt
@TestMethodOrder(MethodOrderer.OrderAnnotation::class)
class ExampleControllerTest {
    private val ctx = mockk<Context>(relaxed = true)
    
    private val ec: ExampleController = ExampleController()

    @Test
    @Order(1)
    fun `this test will break`() {
        every {
            ctx.body()
        } returns jacksonObjectMapper().writeValueAsString(ExampleBody("this is my data"))

        ec.thisWillBreak(ctx)

        verify {
            ctx.status(200)
        }
    }
}
```

**Additional context**
- Javalin/testtools 5.6.1
- Mockk 1.13.5
- Assertj 3.24.2

This is a complete shot in the dark but it could it possibly be related to the fact it's using a data class? I'm not entirely sure, any help would be greatly appreciated. If there is a better way to do this, or a way that doesn't break everything please also update the [article](https://javalin.io/tutorials/testing) for implmenting testing using mockk. Thank you!

